@*
    A story-point estimate, drawn as a badge rather than as a value.

    Effort reads at a glance the way a status does — it is the number a reader
    scans a list of items for — so it wears the application's badge instead of
    sitting quietly among the authored scalars. Its own tone, because an estimate
    is not a state: `effort` is the badge kind, and nothing about it says whether
    the work has started.

    The unit is spelled out. "5" on its own does not say what it counts, and the
    one thing a reader must not do with a story-point count is read it as days.

    Absent when there is no estimate, and present when the estimate is zero. Those
    are different answers — nobody has sized this, against sized and found to be
    nothing — and a component that collapsed them would lose the sizing.

    The unit is also why the row this sits in needs no visible label: "5 pts" names
    its own field, which a bare "5" never could. The tooltip says the field name for
    a pointer user, since the `dt` is hidden there.
*@

@if (Effort is int effort)
{
    <Badge Kind="effort" Title="@TitleValue" TestId="@TestId" CssClass="@CssClass">@effort pts</Badge>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The estimate. Null is "not estimated" and draws nothing; 0 is an
    /// estimate and draws its badge.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int? Effort { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The tooltip. Defaulted to the field name and the unit spelled out,
    /// because the row this is drawn in hides its label and the one thing a reader
    /// must not do with a story-point count is read it as days.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The test id on the badge. Defaulted rather than left to the
    /// caller: the estimate is drawn as a badge and not as text, so a test looking
    /// for the number has nothing else to find it by.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; } = "knowledge-effort-badge";

    /// <summary>A modifier on top of the badge's own classes.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    private string TitleValue =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Title) ? $"effort: {Effort} story points" : Title;
}
